<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Banu Kanz</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Banu Kanz were an Arab tribal dynasty of the Aswan region on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s southern frontier, descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabia">Rabi'a</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> who settled there in the 9th century and intermarried with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubians">Nubians</a>. Their leaders bore the title Kanz al-Dawla, granted by the Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>. In the early 14th century Kanz al-Dawla, who claimed the throne of Makuria through descent from its royal family, fought for the crown, and the Banu Kanz furthered the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Arab tribes, the largest of them the Mudhar, Rabi'ah, Juhaynah and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a> 'Aylan, moved to the Egyptian frontier with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> in the 9th century after gold and emerald mines were found there. In 855 Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hamid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-umari">al-Umari</a>, a native of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, came to Aswan to profit from the gold mines and built a virtually independent principality on them, twice defeating the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-tulun">Ahmad ibn Tulun</a>, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, before he was assassinated by Mudhar tribesmen.</p><p>The Rabi'ah then became the strongest of the frontier tribes, running a principality of their own by the 10th century in alliance with the Hadariba, a Muslim tribe of the Beja, with whom they shared the mining business. Abu Yazid ibn Ishaq made Aswan its capital and was recognised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> as protector of Aswan. In 1006 his son Abu al-Makarim Hibatallah received the title Kanz al-Dawla, Treasure of the State, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> al-Hakim for capturing the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-rakwa">Abu Rakwa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The principality took in the countryside of Aswan, the frontier with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> and most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-desert">Eastern Desert</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>, with the mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadi-allaqi">Wadi Allaqi</a> and the routes to the port of Aydhab. The Kanz al-Dawla answered to the Fatimid governor of Qus and handled relations and trade with Nubia, frontier taxes and the protection of caravans.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> ended the Fatimid caliphate, the Banu Kanz first helped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> drive a Nubian invasion out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> in 1171-1172, but when their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> were given to an Ayyubid emir they killed him. In 1174 the Kanz al-Dawla Ibn al-Mutawwaj rose to restore the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>; he was defeated at Aswan, captured and executed, and the Banu Kanz moved south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Maris</a>, where they took up Nubian culture and language while remaining Muslim.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1317 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan an-Nasir Muhammad arrested the Kanz al-Dawla, a nephew of King Karanbas of Makuria, but he was soon released and seized the throne, holding it against two Mamluk expeditions, the last in 1324. Later in the century the Banu Kanz attacked Aswan in 1366 and burned it in 1370. Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barquq">Barquq</a> sent the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawwara">Hawwara</a> tribesmen against them, and the Hawwara gradually replaced them. Their descendants, the Kunuz, live in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Banu Kanz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Barshambu was soon overthrown, and Kanz al-Dawla, leader of the <b>Banu Kanz</b> of the Aswan region, who claimed the throne through descent from the royal family, contested the crown in the following years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/makuria">Makuria · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Banu Kanz</b> descended from Arabs of the Rabi'a tribe who settled in the Aswan region and the gold mines of the Eastern Desert in the 9th century and intermarried with Nubians and Beja.</blockquote><a href="/state/banu-kanz-emirate">Banu Kanz emirate</a></li></ul></section>
